.\" The manual page of subedit-gui.
.\"
.\" **It exists because Gaupol has one** -- issue #272. The page of subedit-cli
.\" said the opposite in one sentence of SEE ALSO: "a window is not described
.\" by a manual page". That was a gap dressed up as a decision, and the
.\" counterexample was in the reference repository: the only document Gaupol
.\" installs is a manual page, and it is the one of its graphical program.
.\"
.\" What a page describes of a graphical application is not its window: it is
.\" its command-line surface, which exists and is held here. The chapters of
.\" the window stay in the full manual.
.\"
.\" **The last page written by hand.** The pages of subedit-cli are generated
.\" from its manual since #683 (src/scripts/md-to-man.py); this one is still a
.\" template, configured at installation. Like them, it is written in ASCII
.\" from end to end: troff reads bytes and not UTF-8 as long as nobody calls
.\" preconv for it. check-installation.sh renders every installed page with
.\" "man -l --warnings=w" (check-man-pages.py) and refuses the slightest warning.
.TH SUBEDIT-GUI 1 "" "subedit @PROJECT_VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
subedit\-gui \- edit subtitle files in a window
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B subedit\-gui
.RB [ \-\-version ]
.RI [ file ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B subedit\-gui
opens a window on a subtitle file, or on nothing. It reads SubRip and WebVTT
files, recognising the format from the content rather than the extension and
the encoding from the bytes, and shares its core with
.BR subedit\-cli (1):
the two agree on what a file is and on what an operation does.
.PP
.B One file, and one only.
Further arguments are ignored \(em the window holds a single project at a time.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version on the standard output and exit at once, without opening a
window.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B A file that cannot be opened does not stop the program.
The window opens empty and the reason is written on the standard error, in the
same words
.BR subedit\-cli (1)
uses:
.IR "does not exist" ,
.IR "cannot be opened: permission denied" ,
.IR "cannot be read" ,
.IR "cannot be decoded in the chosen encoding" ,
.IR "is in no format this tool knows" ,
.IR "holds nothing recognisable as a subtitle" .
.PP
A malformed but readable file opens, and whatever the reading recovered on the
way is shown under the table.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The window opened and was closed normally, or
.B \-\-version
answered. A file that failed to open does not change this: the window opened,
and that is what the status reports.
.TP
.B 2
An unexpected failure before the window opened \(em Qt missing, no display
available.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B QT_QPA_PLATFORM
Chooses what the window runs on.
.B If it is set, it is honoured without discussion.
If it is not, the program picks one in a single case: a Wayland session with an
X server beside it, where it asks for
.IR xcb ,
which is what lets the video player draw inside the window.
.I offscreen
runs the window where there is no display server at all.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Where the settings file lives. Defaults to
.I ~/.config
when unset.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/subedit/settings.conf
The settings, written when the window closes. A settings file that cannot be
read costs the settings and nothing else: the window opens on the defaults and
says what it could not read.
.TP
.I @SUBEDIT_MANUAL_DIR@
The complete manual, in Markdown. It is exhaustive where this page is not, and
the window opens it itself under
.BR "Help > Manual" .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR subedit\-cli (1),
the batch tool this program shares its core with, and the manual named above.
